markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Scenario Check: Navigating Unit 3

**Directions:**  
The following scenarios represent common situations you may encounter while supporting students using AI for coding. For each scenario, select the facilitation move that best supports the prompting process and helps students follow a structured workflow.

---

## Question 1: Jumping straight to prompting

<img src="https://images.code.org/ee8319f2ee146806aafaabaaf4747742-image-1778008864048.png"  
     alt="student quickly asking AI without reading code"  
     style="width: 30%; border-radius: 10px; margin: 0px 40px 10px 40px; float: right;">

**Scenario:**  
A student immediately opens the AI tool and types, “Fix my code” without reading the starter code or running the program.

When you ask what the code is doing, they respond, “I don’t know. That’s why I’m asking AI.”

What is the best facilitation move?

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
A) Ask them to explain what the code is doing before using AI
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Correct Answer:</strong> Redirect the student to first understand the starter code. Ask guiding questions like, “What do you think this function does?” or “What happens when you run it?” This builds the foundation needed for effective prompting.
</div>
</details>

<br>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
B) Let them use AI since it can explain the code for them
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Incorrect Answer:</strong> AI can help explain code, but students still need to slow down and build their own understanding before asking for a fix.
</div>
</details>

<br>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
C) Give them the correct code so they can move forward
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Incorrect Answer:</strong> Giving the answer may help them finish the task, but it skips the important step of understanding the starter code and learning how to ask better questions.
</div>
</details>

<br>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
D) Tell them to skip the task if they are confused
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Incorrect Answer:</strong> Confusion is a normal part of coding. Students should be guided back to the code and supported in making sense of it.
</div>
</details>

---
---

## Question 2: Jumping to AI Without a Plan

<img src="https://images.code.org/21e4914ec8b70aeca718efbda927e545-image-1778009101447.png"  
     alt="student typing vague prompt into AI"  
     style="width: 30%; border-radius: 10px; margin: 0px 40px 10px 40px; float: right;">

**Scenario:**  
A student finds the TODO comment and immediately types, “Write this for me” into the AI.

They haven’t described what they want the program to do and seem unsure of the expected behavior.

What should you do?




<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
A) Let them try the prompt and fix it later
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Incorrect Answer:</strong> While refining is part of the process, students should first practice planning what they want the code to do before asking AI to generate code.
</div>
</details>

<br>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
B) Provide the exact prompt they should use
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Incorrect Answer:</strong> Providing the exact prompt may get them unstuck, but it does not help them develop the skill of planning and prompting independently.
</div>
</details>

<br>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
C) Tell them to skip the TODO and move on
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Incorrect Answer:</strong> TODO comments are there to guide students toward the specific place where code needs to be added or changed.
</div>
</details>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
D) Ask them to describe the behavior in their own words first
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Correct Answer:</strong> Guide the student to plan the behavior before prompting. Have them explain what the program should do step-by-step in plain language to build clarity and intention.
</div>
</details>

<br>

---
---

## Question 3: Weak Prompting

<img src="https://images.code.org/fbabed48f7f4180da9189bda0a8c233d-image-1778009282565.png"  
     alt="student writing vague prompt"  
     style="width: 30%; border-radius: 10px; margin: 0px 40px 10px 40px; float: right;">

**Scenario:**  
A student writes the prompt: “Sort this array.”

The AI returns code, but it doesn’t match what the student needs. The student says, “AI is wrong.”

What is the best facilitation move?

<div style="clear:both;"></div>



<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
A) Help them revise the prompt with more detail using context and goal
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Correct Answer:</strong> Guide the student to improve their prompt by adding context, goal, constraints, and output expectations. For example, they could describe what is in the array, what value should be sorted, and whether the order should be smallest to largest.
</div>
</details>

<br>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
B) Tell them the AI is unreliable and they should stop using it
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Incorrect Answer:</strong> The issue may not be the AI itself. A vague prompt often leads to a vague or mismatched response.
</div>
</details>

<br>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
C) Fix the code for them so they can move on
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Incorrect Answer:</strong> Fixing it for the student skips the opportunity to help them improve the prompt and understand why the output did not match their goal.
</div>
</details>

<br>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
D) Have them keep submitting the same prompt again
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;font-size: 12pt;">
<strong>Incorrect Answer:</strong> Repeating the same vague prompt is unlikely to improve the result. Students should revise the prompt with more specific information.
</div>
</details>

---
---


MARKDOWN
